### Benchmark 660

To evaluate the expression generation ability of H2O from histopathology images, we benchmarked it against 661 four  state-of-the-art  methods,  including  OmiCLIP,  HisToGene, BLEEP, and DeepPT. Together  with  H2O, 662 these five approaches were systematically trained and assessed on the two HEST-1k subsets, including HEST663 1k-ccRCC and HEST-1k-PRAD, using the four evaluation metrics defined above. To further examine the 664 generalizability of these methods, we additionally tested them on the additional HEST-1k-IDC-LymphNode 665 dataset, using models pretrained on the HEST-1k-ccRCC and HEST-1k-PRAD benchmarks. 666

HisToGene. HisToGene  was  originally  designed  to  operate  on  112×112  image  patches.  To  ensure  fair 667 comparison with other methods, where the patch size directly influences the amount of information conveyed, 668 we adapted HisToGene to use 224×224 patches while keeping the rest of the architecture unchanged. 669

- BLEEP. BLEEP leverages contrastive learning to construct a low-dimensional joint embedding space from a 670 reference dataset containing paired image and gene expression profiles at micrometer resolution. It employs a 671 ResNet50 pretrained on ImageNet as the image encoder and a fully connected network as the expression 672 encoder. We adopted the preprocessing pipeline and architectural framework of BLEEP. The training set for 673 training or fine-tuning other methods are used as the reference dataset for BLEEP. 674

DeepPT. DeepPT represents the first stage of the ENLIGHT-DeepPT framework, which predicts genome675 wide tumor mRNA expression directly from histopathology slides. In this study, we adapted DeepPT for 676 application to ST data. DeepPT employs a ResNet-50 backbone pretrained on ImageNet as the image encoder. 677 To reduce the dynamic range of expression values and mitigate batch- and experiment-specific discrepancies 678 in library size, we followed the normalization scheme described in the original DeepPT paper. 679

